# Rust patterns for Pier
Style, lints, naming and docs live in style.mdc; tests in testing.mdc. This file covers structure only.

## Crates and boundaries
- Packages are `pier-core`, `pier-api-types`, `pier-server`, `pier-web` (folders `crates/core`, ...).
- `pier-core` has no HTTP or DB dependency and returns its own `model::*` types, never bollard types.
  `pier-api-types` holds the wire types (serde only, WASM-safe, `deny_unknown_fields` on requests).
  `pier-server` converts core -> api types with plain functions in `api/dto.rs` (both crates are
  foreign to it, so no `From` impls). `pier-web` depends on `pier-api-types` only.
- All Docker access goes through `pier_core::docker::DockerApi`. No bollard calls outside core.

## Docker wrapper
- `DockerApi` (async-trait) is the interface; `DockerClient` is the bollard implementation, split per
  resource in `docker/{networks,volumes,containers,images}.rs` as inherent `<kind>_<op>` methods that
  the trait impl in `docker/mod.rs` delegates to. `FakeDocker` (`test-util` feature) is the test double.
- Validate first, then connect: every method calls `pier_core::validate::*` before touching bollard
  (names and ids end up in URL paths). The server validates again at the edge to return 400 early.
- bollard client is created lazily (`DockerClient::docker()`); a missing socket is `Unavailable`, not a
  startup failure. HTTP 304 from start/stop is success. Create requests carry only image/name/cmd.
- Connection settings: `ConnectionConfig` -> `Endpoint::parse` (pure, testable) -> `connection::connect`
  (the only place that applies platform limits: unix sockets and ssh behind `cfg(unix)`, named pipes
  behind `cfg(windows)`; the unsupported branch returns `InvalidConfig`). `Endpoint::parse` itself is
  platform-independent so every scheme is unit-tested on every OS.

## Errors
- Library: one `pier_core::CoreError` (alias `Error`) with coarse variants (InvalidInput, NotFound,
  Conflict, Forbidden, Unavailable, InvalidConfig, Docker); `From<bollard::errors::Error>` classifies.
- Server: `ApiError` wraps `CoreError` plus auth/HTTP cases. `core_status()` in `server/src/error.rs` is
  the only CoreError -> status mapping. 5xx bodies are generic (details go to `tracing`). Binaries use
  `anyhow`; the HTTP layer never does.

## Server state and handlers
- `AppState { docker: Arc<dyn DockerApi>, db: SqlitePool, config: Arc<Config>, limiter: Arc<LoginLimiter> }`
  is the only axum state (the trait object, not `Arc<DockerClient>`, is what lets tests inject a fake).
- Handlers are thin: validate -> `state.docker.<op>()` -> convert with `dto::*`. Request bodies use
  `ApiJson<T>` (JSON error body, `deny_unknown_fields` -> 422).
- Router: public routes, protected routes behind `route_layer(require_auth)`, `csrf_guard` on the whole
  `/api` tree, `/api` fallback is a JSON 404, everything else falls to `assets::serve` (SPA fallback that
  never answers `/api/*`). `build_app(state)` in `lib.rs` lets tests drive the real router with `oneshot`.
- Auth: argon2id via `spawn_blocking`; unknown users verify a dummy hash; sessions store the SHA-256 of
  the token; the first admin is inserted with one `INSERT ... WHERE NOT EXISTS` statement.
- Config: `FileConfig` (pier.toml, unknown keys rejected) + injected env closure -> `Config::resolve`.

## Platform and features
- Platform code behind `cfg(unix)` / `cfg(windows)` / `cfg(target_os)` in small private functions; the
  default build and `cargo check --target x86_64-pc-windows-gnu` must stay green.
- Future: streams (logs, stats, events) use one upstream Docker stream per container fanned out through
  `tokio::sync::broadcast`, started on first subscriber and stopped after the last.

## Web (Leptos CSR)
- All calls go through `web/src/api.rs` (JSON content type always set; 401 redirects to /login).
- Pages own their signals and a `refresh` counter that re-runs the `LocalResource`; create forms take
  one argument per line for commands (no shell parsing).
